WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:06.887
"Centrum Multivitamin, Vitamin C, Beta Carotene,
Souvenaid, Zinc, or Calcium Supplements
to Prevent Alzheimer's"

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.140
Alzheimer's patients have
significantly lower blood levels

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.600
of 8 out of 10 antioxidants
investigated,

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.060
though, of course,
this could just be a consequence

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.100
of demented individuals
eating diets

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.600
poor in fruits and vegetables.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.820
When put to the test,

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.760
the PREADViSE study
found no benefit

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.740
for vitamin E or selenium

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.060
in terms of
preventing dementia.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.460
What about other antioxidants?

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.220
In the Harvard Physician's
Health Study,

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:33.960
nearly 6,000 older men

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.720
were randomized
to beta carotene or placebo.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.080
No difference was found
after three years,

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.160
but after 11 years,

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.020
those unknowingly taking
the equivalent

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.060
of 3/4 cup (150 g)
of sweet potato

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.620
worth of beta carotene a day

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:49.200
had slightly
yet significantly better

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:50.700
overall cognitive performance.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:52.920
A similar study of older women

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.700
found no cognitive benefit
over a period of nine years.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.980
Maybe they just didn't wait
long enough?

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.280
Vitamin E and C
were also tested.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:06.240
Vitamin E similarly flopped
throughout and so did vitamin C,

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.840
up until the last cognitive
assessment at around year 9

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.540
where the vitamin C group
appeared to pull ahead.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:17.640
However, two trials of
a combination of vitamin C, E,

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.220
and beta carotene together
versus placebo,

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:23.340
one randomizing
1,500 older patients

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.280
for seven years,
and another truly massive one

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.280
randomizing more than
20,000 adults for five years,

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.100
both failed to show
any cognitive benefit

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.080
for the antioxidant cocktail.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.780
What about a multivitamin?

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.120
The Physician's Health Study
also tested

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.400
taking the daily
multivitamin/multimineral

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.980
supplement Centrum Silver
for 12 years.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:46.620
Compared to those instead
randomized to a placebo,

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:51.349
the Centrum group experienced
no cognitive benefit.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.660
Souvenaid is a nutritional drink

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.060
containing a patented
formulation of nutrients,

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:58.980
branded as Fortasyn Connect,

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.380
specifically designed to help
in Alzheimer's disease.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.500
Unfortunately,
it doesn't work.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.600
There have been three
randomized controlled trials

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.280
enrolling more
than a thousand people,

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.580
and those taking Souvenaid
were no less likely

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.200
to develop dementia
in the first place,

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.360
and probably it has
little or no effect

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.460
on measures of memory
or other thinking skills

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.440
in both those
with mild cognitive impairment

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:20.880
or Alzheimer's disease.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:25.260
In terms of specific minerals
other than the failed selenium,

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.860
zinc has similarly flopped
to improve overall cognition

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.860
over months or years
of supplementation,

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.440
and it's possible
calcium supplements

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.420
could make things worse.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.200
Cross-sectionally,
those taking calcium

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.740
have more "hyperintensity"
brain lesions on MRI

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.280
(shown here in red, denoting
evidence of mini-strokes)

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.620
and longitudinally over time,

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.480
women taking
calcium supplements

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.960
appear to have twice the odds
of developing dementia.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:58.200
The Women's Health Initiative
should have been able

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.360
to settle the question
once and for all.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.700
Thousands of older women
were randomized

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:04.500
to calcium supplements
(plus vitamin D)

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.840
or placebo
for about eight years.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.200
Thankfully, there was
no difference in the risk

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.300
of developing cognitive
impairment or dementia.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.820
But that's what
was originally found

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:16.440
for heart attack
and stroke rates

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.260
before pre-existing
supplement use

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:20.340
was taken into account.

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.740
See, more than half of the women

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.560
were already taking
personal calcium supplements

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:26.700
before entering the study

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.740
and being randomized
to calcium or placebo.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.740
You can imagine how this
could muddy the results.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:33.960
If you just look at the women

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:35.880
who weren't
on calcium beforehand,

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.900
starting calcium resulted
in a significant increase

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.240
in the risk
of cardiovascular events,

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.240
consistent with a meta-analysis
of calcium trials

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:48.060
showing a 15 percent increase
in heart attacks or strokes

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.160
among those randomized
to calcium supplements

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:51.120
over placebo.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.420
Unfortunately,
such a reanalysis

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:57.120
has yet to be performed
on the dementia data.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:01.140
The bottom line is that vitamin
and mineral supplements

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.780
probably aren't going to help
prevent dementia or treat it,

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.720
but there are tons
of other things we can do.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.580
Preserving your Mind

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:11.460
is the largest chapter
of my book How Not to Age.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.800
Check it out
at your local public library

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.320
or listen to me read it
on audiobook.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:19.380
All proceeds I receive
from the sales of all my books

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:20.820
go to charity.

